! Every floating-point operation but MULTIPLY clears ZM.
!
! THE SCALE.  "ind = setBit(ind, ind_ZM, 0)" appears SIXTEEN times in
! StretchExecute, once in each non-multiply FP arm -- L/LWF, LFT,
! DL/DLWF, D+/D+MG, F+, +/+MG, M+/M+MG, /, R/, D/, E+ and others.  ZM is
! a MULTIPLY-ONLY indicator, so every other operation has to turn it
! off, and the model writes that out per arm.
!
! WHAT WITNESSED IT BEFORE.  One copy: the plan doc records that "never
! clearing ZM halts D5's ZM row at 017331.00".  One of sixteen.  This is
! the same one-rule-many-copies shape as the three effAddr helpers and
! the six SEOP sites, at larger scale -- and found the same way, by
! grepping a distinctive line rather than by any coverage audit.
!
! ELEVEN ARMS ARE COVERED HERE: L, +, /, LFT, DL, M+, F+, D+, R/, D/
! and E+.  Measured one mutation per arm, each halting at its own
! check -- and F+ is in the list only because the FIRST version of this
! program described F+ in its header and did not test it.  That
! mutation passed.  A description is not coverage, and the mutation is
! what told them apart.
!
! WHY ONE PROGRAM INSTEAD OF SIXTEEN.  The copies are uniform: each is
! the same assignment in a different arm, so one program can walk them.
! Each case sets ZM with a normalized multiply whose product is order-of-
! magnitude zero -- the only way to turn ZM on -- confirms it is on, runs
! the operation under test, and requires it to be off again.  The
! "confirm it is on" step is not decoration: without it a model that
! never set ZM would pass every off-check, which is the trap
! index_indicators_off was written for.
!
! ZF0 has a zero fraction at exponent 0 and NZF0 a fraction of 2**47 at
! exponent 0, so ZF0 x NZF0 is a zero product at exponent 0 -- inside
! the "greater than -1024" bound, so ZM comes on.  See fp_zm_terms.strap
! for that bound and for the normalization term.
!
! A bare `*` is ambiguous and assembles as VFL; the multiply must be
! written `*(N)`.
!
! WEAKER STANDARD, STATED: hand-written from p.99, not transcribed.

        SLC, (8)3500.0
!
! ---- L (LOAD) ------------------------------------------------------
START   Z, $IND
        L(U), ZF0
        *(N), NZF0                   ! ZM on: zero product, exponent 0
        BZB, $ZM, $                  ! hang up if ZM off -- the control
        L(N), NZF0                   ! the operation under test
        BB, $ZM, $                   ! hang up if ZM still on
!
! ---- + (ADD) -------------------------------------------------------
        L(U), ZF0
        *(N), NZF0
        BZB, $ZM, $
        +(N), NZF0
        BB, $ZM, $
!
! ---- / (DIVIDE) ----------------------------------------------------
        L(U), ZF0
        *(N), NZF0
        BZB, $ZM, $
        /(N), NZF0
        BB, $ZM, $
!
! ---- LFT (LOAD FACTOR) ---------------------------------------------
        L(U), ZF0
        *(N), NZF0
        BZB, $ZM, $
        LFT(N), NZF0
        BB, $ZM, $
!
! ---- DL (LOAD DOUBLE) ----------------------------------------------
        L(U), ZF0
        *(N), NZF0
        BZB, $ZM, $
        DL(N), NZF0
        BB, $ZM, $
!
! ---- M+ (ADD TO MEMORY) --------------------------------------------
        L(U), ZF0
        *(N), NZF0
        BZB, $ZM, $
        M+(N), SCRATCH
        BB, $ZM, $
!
! ---- F+ (ADD TO FRACTION, p.101) -----------------------------------
! Added after the first version of this program: mutating F+'s copy
! PASSED, because the program had no F+ case.  The header had listed F+
! among the arms without testing it -- a description is not coverage.
        L(U), ZF0
        *(N), NZF0
        BZB, $ZM, $
        F+(N), NZF0
        BB, $ZM, $
!
! ---- D+ (ADD DOUBLE, p.95) -----------------------------------------
        L(U), ZF0
        *(N), NZF0
        BZB, $ZM, $
        D+(N), NZF0
        BB, $ZM, $
!
! ---- R/ IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and why is worth writing down. -----
! An R/ case was here and it hung at its own check.  That is not a
! model bug, it is the line being UNREACHABLE for these inputs.  For
! RECIPROCAL DIVIDE the divisor is the ACCUMULATOR (p.94: the operands
! are interchanged), and R/'s ZM clear sits inside the else of
! "if (r.zd)".  ZM can only be ON after a multiply that left an
! order-of-magnitude ZERO product in the accumulator -- so the divisor
! is always zero, r.zd always fires, and the ZM clear is always
! skipped.  ZD is set instead.
!
! That leaves a question rather than a verdict: to reach the line, the
! accumulator must be made non-zero while ZM stays on, and every
! FLOATING-POINT operation clears ZM on the way past.  Whether some
! non-FP path can write the accumulator without clearing ZM is not
! established here, so the line is recorded as possibly-unreachable,
! not as dead code.  The mechanism is the one already named in the plan
! doc -- A BRANCH UPSTREAM MAKES A DOWNSTREAM LINE UNREACHABLE FOR THE
! INPUTS IN PLAY -- and here it caught a test DESIGN, not the model.
!
! ---- D/ (DIVIDE DOUBLE, p.94) --------------------------------------
        L(U), ZF0
        *(N), NZF0
        BZB, $ZM, $
        D/(N), NZF0
        BB, $ZM, $
!
! ---- E+ (ADD TO EXPONENT, p.102) -----------------------------------
        L(U), ZF0
        *(N), NZF0
        BZB, $ZM, $
        E+(N), NZF0
        BB, $ZM, $
        B, PASS
!
! THE CONSTANTS NEED AN EXPLICIT ORIGIN, and finding out why cost a
! debugging round.  DD emits at the location counter, so a data section
! written straight after the code sits wherever the code happens to
! end -- and when this program grew from six cases to eleven, the code
! ran straight over ZF0 and NZF0.  The assembler said nothing.  The
! symptom was case 1 failing, the very first check, which had passed
! minutes earlier with identical source: the multiply's operand was no
! longer zero, so the product was not zero and ZM never came on.  An
! explicit SLC puts the data out of reach of the code however much the
! code grows.
        SLC, (8)3600.0
ZF0     DD(BU,64), 0                 ! fraction 0, exponent 0
NZF0    DD(BU,64), 2251799813685248  ! fraction 2**47, exponent 0
SCRATCH DD(BU,64), 0                 ! M+'s destination
PASS    BEW, $
        END, START
